WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.720
Attributes combine syntax with behavior.

2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.391
Text-like attributes such as title,
aria-label,

3
00:00:05.471 --> 00:00:09.788
and controlled data attributes can receive
values through fixed DOM properties or

4
00:00:09.868 --> 00:00:11.533
safe attribute serialization.

5
00:00:11.613 --> 00:00:14.944
Even there, keep the attribute name under
application control.

6
00:00:15.024 --> 00:00:19.341
An API that safely serializes a value does
not make a user-selected event attribute

7
00:00:19.421 --> 00:00:21.456
or other active attribute acceptable.

8
00:00:21.536 --> 00:00:24.496
URL-bearing properties such as href, src,
form actions,

9
00:00:24.576 --> 00:00:26.920
and resource locations add semantic
policy.

10
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.590
Parse a candidate with a standards-aware
URL API,

11
00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:33.864
account deliberately for relative URLs by
supplying a trusted base when they are

12
00:00:33.944 --> 00:00:37.583
allowed, and permit only the protocols and
origins the feature needs.

13
00:00:37.663 --> 00:00:41.672
A URL can be syntactically valid and
safely assigned to a property while still

14
00:00:41.752 --> 00:00:44.342
navigating or loading from a forbidden
location.

15
00:00:44.422 --> 00:00:47.074
Percent encoding protects component
boundaries;

16
00:00:47.154 --> 00:00:50.670
it does not decide whether a protocol or
destination is authorized.

17
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:54.265
Inline event-handler attributes are
JavaScript source contexts.

18
00:00:54.345 --> 00:00:57.429
Do not build onclick or similar strings
from external data.

19
00:00:57.509 --> 00:01:01.950
Register a developer-authored function
with addEventListener and let request data

20
00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:03.942
remain data passed to that function.

21
00:01:04.022 --> 00:01:08.400
This approach separates behavior from
markup and avoids a nested language boundary

22
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.564
where HTML parsing hands a string to the
JavaScript engine.

23
00:01:11.644 --> 00:01:14.481
String-to-code functions deserve the same
scrutiny.

24
00:01:14.561 --> 00:01:17.707
Style attributes and style text create CSS
interpretation.

25
00:01:17.787 --> 00:01:21.672
Prefer assigning a known property with a
value selected from an allowlist,

26
00:01:21.752 --> 00:01:25.514
such as a small set of theme tokens,
rather than accepting arbitrary CSS.

27
00:01:25.594 --> 00:01:29.788
Review the property itself because some
values can cause resource loads or other

28
00:01:29.868 --> 00:01:30.423
behavior.

29
00:01:30.503 --> 00:01:34.512
The durable distinction is that output
encoding protects a syntax boundary,

30
00:01:34.592 --> 00:01:38.786
while URL, event, and style policy governs
what the resulting browser feature is

31
00:01:38.866 --> 00:01:39.730
permitted to do.

32
00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.140
Both may be necessary, and neither can
substitute for the other.
